## Spawning Tracked Background Runs

**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.

**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.

Agent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.

Use `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).

`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.

**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:
- task description comes from the element README
- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element
- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD
- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts

Hidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.

If the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.

The prompt also includes:
1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)
2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)
3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)
4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)

**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.

**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.
`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board
lookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.
`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the
only opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes
`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`
once for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider
allowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child
launches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an
optional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a
sanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry
composition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.

**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It
requires `agents-infra compose` to implement
`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a
successful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact
Codex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the
response is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain
fully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with
sanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.

The private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the
detached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under
`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,
commands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve
only MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values
remain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply
primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,
prompt, or goal settings from infra composition.
`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively
execute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.
Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider
before ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set
provider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`
and then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference
usability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.
For each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the
configured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any
`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured
provider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections
use the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.
Optional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent
ceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw
configured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits
report the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.

**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.

**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.

For spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).

